course: dictionary
lesson: 2383
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Cure can be a thing (a noun) or an action (a verb). «Scientists are searching for a cure for the illness.» Here a cure is a medicine or treatment that makes the illness go away. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence.
explain: «Talking to Toad'll never cure him.» Here cure is a verb. It means to make someone well again. In the story the Rat is flat and sad, not ill with a germ, so the cure is about making him happy and well in himself.
explain: Be careful with adverts. «"Miracle" cures» that promise too much are often not real. Real cures are tested by scientists, who publish the results. Someone saying «they're hiding it» is not proof that a cure works.
fact: Cure can be a noun (a cure) or a verb (to cure, cures, cured, curing).
fact: A cure is a medicine or treatment that makes an illness go away completely. To cure someone is to make them well again, and to cure a problem is to solve it.
fact: To cure meat or fish is to keep it from going bad by salting, smoking or drying it, like bacon.
fact: Some illnesses have a cure, but others can only be treated or prevented, for example with vaccines.
fact: Useful phrases: a cure for something, a cure-all, and «prevention is better than cure».
exercise: «Scientists are searching for a cure for the illness.» What does cure mean here? | A bright light that shines in the dark | A song sung at a party | A place where children play | *A medicine or treatment that makes the illness go away
exercise: «Talking to Toad'll never cure him.» What does cure mean here? | *To make him well again | To cook him a meal | To lock him in a box | To cut his hair
exercise: Which phrase means it is better to stop an illness from happening than to treat it? | a cure for | *prevention is better than cure | cure-all | to cure meat
test: In the story, the Rat starts to scribble poetry, and then «the cure has begun». What has begun to cure him? | *Writing poetry | Eating a big winter dinner | Leaving the wood forever | Stopping the rain
test: Which word means to keep meat or fish from going bad by salting, smoking or drying it? | Paint | Bake | *Cure | Polish
exam: A pop-up advert says a cream removes every wrinkle in three days. What is the best reason to doubt it is a real cure? | *Nobody has tested it or published scientific results to show it works | It is a cream | It is shown on a pop-up | It was in the news
